(1) An application for registration must be submitted to the Registrar using the prescribed form.
(2) The application must include:
(a) The full name and address of the owner;
(b) The make, model, and serial number of the equipment;
(c) Proof of ownership, such as a bill of sale or title document;
(d) A declaration stating the primary intended use of the equipment;
(e) A recent photograph of the equipment, not older than 90 days.
(3) A registration fee of 250 crowns is payable upon application.

The Authority may make regulations for the effective administration of this Act, including but not limited to:
(a) prescribing forms for applications and licences;
(b) establishing technical standards for amateur radio equipment;
(c) detailing operating procedures and protocols;
(d) setting specific frequency allocations and usage rules;
(e) defining procedures for emergency communications; and
(f) any other matter necessary for carrying out the provisions of this Act. All regulations must be published in the official gazette at least 30 days before coming into force. A public review period of 45 days will precede final approval.

(1) The following places are highland settlements, each lying within the municipality named:
(a) Skarholt, in the municipality of Grauby, 640 metres above sea level;
(b) Brattli, in the municipality of Grauby, 780 metres above sea level;
(c) Fjellmo, in the municipality of Dunmere, 860 metres above sea level;
(d) Ravnstig, in the municipality of Corvik, 710 metres above sea level.
(2) A person or household lives in a highland settlement when its residence is at an address within that settlement.
(3) Wherever an Act grants relief to persons or households in a highland settlement, it refers to the places listed in subsection (1).

(1) The following places are highland settlements, each lying within the municipality named:
(a) Skarholt, in the municipality of Grauby, 640 metres above sea level;
(b) Brattli, in the municipality of Grauby, 780 metres above sea level;
(c) Fjellmo, in the municipality of Dunmere, 860 metres above sea level;
(d) Ravnstig, in the municipality of Corvik, 710 metres above sea level;
(e) Kolbu, in the municipality of Dunmere, 590 metres above sea level;
(f) Stenvarde, in the municipality of Brenholt, 690 metres above sea level.
(2) A person or household lives in a highland settlement when its residence is at an address within that settlement.
(3) Wherever an Act grants relief to persons or households in a highland settlement, it refers to the places listed in subsection (1).

Any person or entity who:
(a) Uses crop protection products without a permit, as required by section 3;
(b) Knowingly provides false or misleading information in an application or report;
(c) Obstructs an inspector in the performance of their duties;
(d) Fails to comply with an emergency order issued under section 21, commits an offense and is liable to a fine not exceeding 100,000 crowns, or imprisonment for a term not exceeding 1 year, or both, for serious violations. Repeat offenses may incur double penalties.

Any person who:
(a) Provides chimney sweeping services without a valid registration certificate;
(b) Falsifies information in an application for registration or renewal;
(c) Fails to comply with a directive or order issued by the Registrar;
(d) Obstructs the Registrar or an authorized inspector in the performance of their duties; or
(e) Otherwise contravenes any provision of this Act, commits an offense and is liable to the penalties specified in section 35. Each day that an offense continues may be considered a separate offense, incurring additional penalties.

(1) The Nordvale Environmental Agency may revoke a Water Extraction Permit under certain circumstances, including but not limited to:
(a) Persistent failure to pay levies or submit reports;
(b) Gross misrepresentation of extraction volumes;
(c) Significant environmental damage caused by extraction activities;
(d) Repeated breaches of any provision of this Act or permit conditions.
(2) Before revocation, the permit holder will be issued a written notice of intent to revoke, providing 30 days to rectify the issues or present a defense.
(3) If revocation occurs, all extraction activities must cease immediately.

A permit for a drainage system will be approved if the proposed plans meet all of the following criteria:
(a) Compliance with all relevant building codes and safety standards;
(b) Compatibility with the existing municipal drainage infrastructure;
(c) No adverse impact on adjacent properties or the natural environment, as determined by an environmental assessment if required;
(d) Use of approved materials as listed by the National Standards Board of Nordvale;
(e) Proper dimensioning to handle anticipated flow rates, ensuring at least a 15% safety margin.

The following activities are exempt from the permit requirements of this Act:
(a) Routine cleaning of existing drainage pipes, not involving excavation;
(b) Replacement of a single section of pipe less than 2 meters in length with identical materials, provided it does not alter the system's capacity or discharge point;
(c) Emergency repairs as defined in section 12, provided a permit application is submitted within 7 days;
(d) Work performed by municipal utility employees on public infrastructure.
All exempt activities must still comply with all general safety and environmental regulations. These exemptions are intended for minimal impact activities.

Funds from the Municipal Green Space Fund may be used for: 
(a) routine maintenance of public parks and green spaces, including mowing, planting, and waste removal;
(b) repair and replacement of park infrastructure such as benches, play equipment, and pathways;
(c) development of new green spaces and ecological restoration projects;
(d) salaries of personnel directly involved in green space management, up to a maximum of 15% of the annual fund balance;
(e) public consultation and engagement activities related to green space planning.

Funds from the Municipal Green Space Fund may be used for: 
(a) routine maintenance of public parks and green spaces, including mowing, planting, and waste removal;
(b) repair and replacement of park infrastructure such as benches, play equipment, and pathways;
(c) development of new green spaces and ecological restoration projects;
(d) salaries of personnel directly involved in green space management, up to a maximum of 18% of the annual fund balance;
(e) public consultation and engagement activities related to green space planning; and
(f) educational programs promoting green space conservation.

Funds from the Municipal Green Space Fund shall not be used for:
(a) general municipal administrative costs unrelated to green space management;
(b) funding of projects or initiatives not directly related to public green spaces;
(c) political lobbying or campaign activities;
(d) salaries for municipal staff not directly involved in green space maintenance or development;
(e) purchasing land that is not intended for designation as a public green space within 24 months of purchase. This strict prohibition ensures the integrity of the fund's purpose.

A privacy impact assessment must include:
(a) a systematic description of the envisaged processing operations and the purposes of the processing;
(b) an assessment of the necessity and proportionality of the processing operations in relation to the purposes;
(c) an assessment of the risks to the rights and freedoms of individuals;
(d) the measures envisaged to address the risks, including safeguards, security measures, and mechanisms to ensure the protection of personal information and to demonstrate compliance with this Act, taking into account the rights and legitimate interests of individuals and other persons concerned.

An applicant for a Burial Permit must be at least 18 years of age and must be either:
(a) The next-of-kin of the deceased;
(b) An executor of the deceased's will;
(c) A legal representative of the deceased's estate;
(d) A designated funeral director acting on behalf of the family or estate; or
(e) In exceptional circumstances, a public official acting in the public interest, such as in cases of unidentified remains or public health emergencies. The Municipal Authority reserves the right to request additional verification of the applicant's relationship or authority.

Any person or entity who:
(a) Conducts a burial or interment without a valid Burial Permit;
(b) Provides false information on a permit application;
(c) Fails to comply with the terms and conditions of an issued permit;
(d) Obstructs an authorised inspector in the performance of their duties;
(e) Fails to maintain records as required by this Act; commits an offence against this Act. Upon conviction, such a person or entity shall be liable to a fine of not less than 500 crowns and not exceeding 5000 crowns, or imprisonment for a term not exceeding 6 months, or both.

(1) An inspector, upon presenting official identification, may enter any workplace or public facility at any reasonable hour without prior notice for the purpose of conducting an inspection.
(2) Inspectors are empowered to:
(a) Examine any machinery, equipment, or substance;
(b) Request the production of any documents, records, or plans relevant to health and safety;
(c) Interview any person present on the premises;
(d) Take samples of any substance or material for analysis;
(e) Issue immediate prohibition notices if an imminent danger is identified.

(1) An inspector, upon presenting official identification, may enter any workplace or public facility at any reasonable hour, which includes between 06:00 and 22:00, without prior notice for the purpose of conducting an inspection.
(2) Inspectors are empowered to:
(a) Examine any machinery, equipment, or substance, including taking photographs or videos;
(b) Request the production of any documents, records, or plans relevant to health and safety, including digital records;
(c) Interview any person present on the premises, provided the interview does not unduly disrupt operations for more than 15 minutes;
(d) Take samples of any substance or material for analysis, up to a maximum volume of 500 millilitres or 500 grams;
(e) Issue immediate prohibition notices if an imminent danger is identified, effective for a period of up to 72 hours.

(1) Following each inspection, the inspector shall prepare a detailed inspection report within 10 working days.
(2) The report shall include:
(a) Date and time of inspection;
(b) Name of the inspector;
(c) Identification of the premises inspected;
(d) Findings, including any identified non-compliance;
(e) Recommended corrective actions;
(f) Deadline for compliance with corrective actions, typically 30 days for minor issues and 7 days for serious hazards.

(1) Where a contravention of this Act results in serious injury or death, the responsible party may be liable to a fine of up to 200,000 crowns or, in severe cases, imprisonment for a term not exceeding 2 years.
(2) Aggravated offenses include:
(a) Deliberate disregard for safety regulations;
(b) Gross negligence leading to harm;
(c) Repeated failure to address identified hazards, even after multiple compliance orders.
(3) The NHSA may also recommend the revocation of relevant operating licenses for a period of up to 5 years.

(1) Where a contravention of this Act results in serious injury or death, the responsible party may be liable to a fine of up to 300,000 crowns or, in severe cases, imprisonment for a term not exceeding 3 years. This applies to individuals and corporate entities.
(2) Aggravated offenses include:
(a) Deliberate disregard for safety regulations that could foreseeably lead to harm;
(b) Gross negligence leading to harm, defined as a clear and obvious failure to exercise reasonable care;
(c) Repeated failure to address identified hazards, even after multiple compliance orders and at least one prior fine.
(3) The NHSA may also recommend the revocation of relevant operating licenses for a period of up to 10 years, or permanently for repeat offenders.

(1) The owner or operator of any workplace or public facility must immediately report to the NHSA any serious incident that occurs on their premises.
(2) A 'serious incident' is defined as:
(a) Any incident resulting in death or major injury requiring hospitalization for more than 24 hours;
(b) Any dangerous occurrence that had the potential to cause serious harm, such as a major structural collapse or uncontrolled release of hazardous substances;
(c) Any incident causing property damage exceeding 10,000 crowns.
(3) Reports must be made within 8 hours for fatalities or major injuries, and within 48 hours for other serious incidents.

The Board shall have the power and duty to: 
(a) Establish criteria for the issuance, renewal, suspension, and revocation of licences;
(b) Administer examinations to assess competency for licensure;
(c) Investigate complaints against licensees and take disciplinary action as appropriate;
(d) Establish and enforce a code of ethics for psychologists;
(e) Maintain an official register of all licensed psychologists in Nordvale;
(f) Collect all fees associated with licensing applications, examinations, and renewals;
(g) Publish an annual report detailing its activities and any changes in licensing regulations. These powers are critical for ensuring public protection.

(1) To be eligible for a psychologist licence, an applicant must: 
(a) Be at least 21 years of age;
(b) Hold a doctoral degree in psychology from an accredited institution recognised by the Nordvale Ministry of Education;
(c) Have completed a minimum of 1,500 hours of supervised professional experience, as approved by the Board;
(d) Pass the Nordvale Psychological Licensing Examination with a score of 75% or higher;
(e) Demonstrate good moral character and professional fitness, as assessed by the Board. 
(2) All documents submitted must be original or certified copies. The Board may require additional documentation if necessary to verify eligibility.

(1) A psychologist licence is valid for a period of two years from the date of issuance and must be renewed by December 31st of the renewal year. 
(2) To renew a licence, a licensee must:
(a) Submit a completed renewal application form;
(b) Pay a renewal fee of 750 crowns;
(c) Provide proof of completion of at least 40 hours of approved continuing professional development (CPD) activities within the preceding two-year period.
(3) Failure to renew within 30 days of the expiry date will result in a late fee of 150 crowns. A licence not renewed within 90 days will be automatically suspended.

(1) A psychologist licence is valid for a period of two years from the date of issuance and must be renewed by June 30th of the renewal year. 
(2) To renew a licence, a licensee must:
(a) Submit a completed renewal application form;
(b) Pay a renewal fee of 900 crowns;
(c) Provide proof of completion of at least 50 hours of approved continuing professional development (CPD) activities within the preceding two-year period.
(3) Failure to renew within 45 days of the expiry date will result in a late fee of 200 crowns. A licence not renewed within 120 days will be automatically suspended.

(1) A licence suspended for non-renewal may be reinstated within a period of one year from the suspension date by:
(a) Submitting a reinstatement application;
(b) Paying all outstanding renewal fees and a reinstatement fee of 500 crowns;
(c) Providing proof of completion of all required CPD hours, plus an additional 10 hours for each year the licence was suspended.
(2) After one year, a suspended licence is considered revoked, and the individual must reapply for a new licence as per section 7, including re-examination.

(1) Following an investigation, if the Board finds that a psychologist has violated this Act or the Code of Ethics, it may take one or more of the following disciplinary actions: 
(a) Issue a written reprimand;
(b) Impose a fine of up to 10,000 crowns;
(c) Require additional continuing professional development or supervised practice;
(d) Suspend the licence for a specified period, not exceeding two years;
(e) Revoke the licence permanently. 
(2) The severity of the action will be commensurate with the gravity of the violation. The psychologist has a right to appeal the decision as per section 25.

(1) Following an investigation, if the Board finds that a psychologist has violated this Act or the Code of Ethics, it may take one or more of the following disciplinary actions: 
(a) Issue a written reprimand;
(b) Impose a fine of up to 15,000 crowns;
(c) Require additional continuing professional development or supervised practice;
(d) Suspend the licence for a specified period, not exceeding three years;
(e) Revoke the licence permanently. 
(2) The severity of the action will be commensurate with the gravity of the violation. The psychologist has a right to appeal the decision as per section 25. The Board's decision aims to protect the public and maintain professional standards.

(1) An application for registration shall be submitted to the Registrar in the prescribed form.
(2) The application must include:
(a) the legal name and any trade names of the Publishing House;
(b) the principal business address, which must be located within Nordvale;
(c) contact information, including a primary email address and telephone number;
(d) details of the ownership structure, including names and addresses of all owners holding 10% or more of the entity;
(e) a declaration affirming compliance with all relevant Nordvale laws.

(1) Registered Publishing Houses must submit an annual report to the Registrar detailing their publishing activities for the preceding fiscal year.
(2) The report must include:
(a) the total number of new titles published;
(b) the total gross revenue generated from publishing activities;
(c) a list of all titles published, including their ISBNs or equivalent identifiers.
(3) The report is due by March 31st of each year. Late submissions will incur a penalty of 100 crowns per day for up to 30 days.

(1) Registered Publishing Houses must submit an annual report to the Registrar detailing their publishing activities for the preceding fiscal year.
(2) The report must include:
(a) the total number of new titles published, categorised by format (print/digital);
(b) the total gross revenue generated from publishing activities, broken down by category;
(c) a list of all titles published, including their ISBNs or equivalent identifiers, and publication dates.
(3) The report is due by February 28th of each year. Late submissions will incur a penalty of 150 crowns per day for up to 45 days.

(1) The following entities are exempt from registration under this Act:
(a) individual authors publishing their own works without operating as a commercial enterprise;
(b) organisations that publish fewer than 25 unique titles annually and have a gross publishing revenue below 150,000 crowns;
(c) educational institutions publishing academic materials for internal use only.
(2) These exemptions do not apply if the entity engages in commercial distribution to the general public beyond specific internal or academic circles.

(1) The following entities are exempt from registration under this Act:
(a) individual authors publishing their own works without operating as a commercial enterprise;
(b) organisations that publish fewer than 15 unique titles annually and have a gross publishing revenue below 100,000 crowns;
(c) non-profit organisations publishing materials solely for informational purposes without commercial intent.
(2) These exemptions do not apply if the entity engages in commercial distribution to the general public beyond specific internal or academic circles.

(1) The Registrar has the authority to:
(a) request additional information from applicants or registered Publishing Houses;
(b) conduct audits of publishing records to verify compliance with this Act;
(c) issue warnings, penalties, or revoke registrations for non-compliance;
(d) maintain and update the public register;
(e) develop and publish guidelines for the interpretation and implementation of this Act. The Registrar's decisions are binding, subject to appeal as per section 29.

(1) The Registrar may suspend the registration of a Publishing House for a period not exceeding 90 days if:
(a) there is a failure to comply with a request for information within 30 days;
(b) the annual report is not submitted after 60 days past the due date;
(c) a serious breach of any provision of this Act is identified, pending further investigation.
(2) During suspension, the Publishing House may not publish any new titles or engage in significant promotional activities as a registered entity. Notice of suspension will be published in the public register.

(1) The Registrar may suspend the registration of a Publishing House for a period not exceeding 120 days if:
(a) there is a failure to comply with a request for information within 20 days;
(b) the annual report is not submitted after 45 days past the due date;
(c) a serious breach of any provision of this Act is identified, pending further investigation.
(2) During suspension, the Publishing House may not publish any new titles or engage in significant promotional activities as a registered entity. Notice of suspension will be published in the public register.

(1) The Registrar may revoke the registration of a Publishing House if:
(a) false or misleading information was provided during the application process;
(b) there is persistent non-compliance with the provisions of this Act after multiple warnings;
(c) the Publishing House has been dissolved or ceased operations for more than 180 days.
(2) Revocation is a serious measure and will only occur after the Publishing House has been given at least 30 days' notice and an opportunity to address the concerns. A revoked registration will be permanently marked as such in the public register.

(1) The Minister for Municipal Affairs may make regulations for carrying into effect the purposes and provisions of this Act.
(2) Without limiting the generality of subsection (1), the Minister may make regulations:
(a) prescribing forms for applications, notices, and reports;
(b) establishing criteria for the designation of public water features;
(c) setting out procedures for the review of levy assessments and appeals.
These regulations provide the necessary operational details for effective implementation.

(1) Where an Act refers to a category in this section, the amount payable is:
(a) Category K1: motor carriage continuation charge, 20 crowns;
(b) Category K2: operator authorisation prolongation, 15 crowns;
(c) Category K3: motor carriage continuation charge, 10 crowns.
(2) Each amount is the whole amount payable.

(1) Where an Act refers to a category in this section, the amount payable is:
(a) Category K1: motor carriage continuation charge, 25 crowns;
(b) Category K2: operator authorisation prolongation, 18 crowns;
(c) Category K3: motor carriage continuation charge, 12 crowns.
(2) Each amount is the whole amount payable.

Any person or entity who:
(a) Drills a water well without a valid permit;
(b) Fails to comply with any condition of a permit;
(c) Provides false or misleading information in an application or report;
(d) Obstructs an authorized inspector in the performance of their duties; shall be guilty of an offence under this Act. Upon conviction, such person or entity shall be liable to a fine not exceeding 10,000 crowns for a first offence, and not exceeding 25,000 crowns for any subsequent offence, in addition to any other remedies available to the Department. Each day an offence continues constitutes a separate offence. The Department may also order the immediate cessation of drilling and remediation.

Any person or entity who:
(a) Drills a water well without a valid permit;
(b) Fails to comply with any condition of a permit;
(c) Provides false or misleading information in an application or report;
(d) Obstructs an authorized inspector in the performance of their duties; shall be guilty of an offence under this Act. Upon conviction, such person or entity shall be liable to a fine not exceeding 15,000 crowns for a first offence, and not exceeding 35,000 crowns for any subsequent offence, in addition to any other remedies available to the Department. Each day an offence continues constitutes a separate offence. The Department may also order the immediate cessation of drilling, remediation at the offender's expense, and confiscation of drilling equipment.
